  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Focal Point",
    "overview": "**Creative North Star: \"The Focal Point\"**\n\nEverything in Didread serves one fixed point: the spot slightly left of centre where each word lands, with its pivot letter lit in Reading-Lamp Amber and two thin guide ticks holding the column. The eye never moves, so the interface around that point has one job: get there quickly and then get out of the way. The library, the finish screen and the settings are calm frames around that point, not destinations in their own right.\n\nThe mood is calm. Neutrals are warm paper by day and warm dark by night, chrome is quiet and low-contrast, and controls fade away completely while words are playing. Amber appears only where attention belongs: the pivot letter, the one main action, progress, and the finish. The system is flat and soft-edged, built for thumbs on a phone first and keys on a desktop second.\n\nThe system avoids three things. It is not a productivity app, so no gradients, glows, glassmorphism or dashboard chrome. It is not a game, so no streaks, badges, confetti or leaderboards; finishing is a quiet win. And it is never cold, so no blue-greys, pure black or neutral grey palettes.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "One fixed focal column (pivot at 40% across), held by guide ticks.",
      "A single accent, Reading-Lamp Amber, used sparingly and always with meaning.",
      "Warm-tinted neutrals in both themes; theme follows the system, with a light/dark override.",
      "Flat surfaces; depth comes from paper versus surface tone and hairline borders, never shadows.",
      "Soft, thumb-sized controls (2.75rem minimum), with exactly one filled amber action per screen.",
      "Controls fade while playing (300ms) and return on pause."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The One Light Rule",
        "body": "Amber is the only chromatic colour in the interface. If something is amber, it is either the focal point, the next action, or progress. Never use it as decoration.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Warm Neutral Rule",
        "body": "Every neutral is tinted towards amber (hue about 75–85 in OKLCH). No `#000`, `#fff` text on a dark ground, or blue-grey anywhere. Pure white appears only as the light-theme surface and as text on amber.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Earned Display Rule",
        "body": "Bricolage is used only for the brand name and for a number that celebrates the reader (time saved, finish stats). Headings for interface sections stay in small uppercase Figtree labels.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Tabular Numbers Rule",
